00:39:57.840 --> 00:40:03.820
And he walks in, he comes up to me and he might be in his forties and he's sweating and he's
他走进来，朝我走过来，他大概四十多岁，满头大汗，还

1185
00:40:03.820 --> 00:40:08.000
got like a backpack on and he takes it off and it, I can see it's heavy.
背着个背包，他把它脱下来，我看得出来挺重的。

1186
00:40:08.040 --> 00:40:08.980
And I'm like, what are you doing?
我就问，你在干嘛？

1187
00:40:08.980 --> 00:40:10.800
He's like, I'm like, you're hiking.
他说，我说，你在徒步旅行吗？

1188
00:40:10.940 --> 00:40:12.080
He's like, no, it's a rucksack.
他说，不，这是重装背包。

1189
00:40:12.080 --> 00:40:15.520
It's a rucksack and it's like this camouflaged backpack.
这是个重装背包，就像这种迷彩双肩包。

1190
00:40:16.280 --> 00:40:19.080
And he says, it has weights in it.
他说，里面有负重。

1191
00:40:19.160 --> 00:40:20.960
It comes and you put weights in it and you walk with it.
买来之后往里面放重物，然后背着它走路。

1192
00:40:21.040 --> 00:40:22.260
You're like hiking with the backpack on.
就像背着背包徒步一样。

1193
00:40:22.280 --> 00:40:23.720
It's like the women with the weighted veSt.
就像女人们穿的那种负重背心。

1194
00:40:23.840 --> 00:40:24.560
I see it everywhere.
我到处都能看到。

1195
00:40:24.820 --> 00:40:24.960
Yeah, but it's a backpack.
对，但这就是个背包。

1196
00:40:25.160 --> 00:40:25.360
Okay.
好吧。

1197
00:40:27.420 --> 00:40:30.360
And he proceeds to pitch this thing to me.
接着他开始向我极力推荐这玩意儿。

1198
00:40:30.460 --> 00:40:31.380
He's very excited about it.
他对此感到非常兴奋。

1199
00:40:31.520 --> 00:40:32.980
By the way, it was 50 pounds.
顺便说一句，这东西有50磅重。

1200
00:40:33.080 --> 00:40:34.000
I can barely lift it.
我几乎提都提不动。

1201
00:40:34.120 --> 00:40:35.400
I'm like, what is going on?
我当时就想，这到底是怎么回事？

1202
00:40:35.460 --> 00:40:35.880
He's like, yep.
他就像是，嗯哼。

1203
00:40:35.880 --> 00:40:37.220
And I like, Hey man.
然后我心想，嘿，老兄。

1204
00:40:37.320 --> 00:40:43.040
And I put my hand on the shoulder and he felt like a rock and he proceeded to speak for 30
然后我把手搭在他肩膀上，他感觉硬得像块石头，接着他滔滔不绝地讲了

1205
00:40:43.040 --> 00:40:45.420
minutes straight about this thing he's doing.
整整30分钟关于他在做的事。

1206
00:40:46.500 --> 00:40:50.780
So we get home and I'm like, Ursula, order me a rucksack, 20 pounds to start.
所以我们到家后我就说，厄休拉，给我订个背包，先从20磅开始。

1207
00:40:50.960 --> 00:40:53.980
And I don't know why, but now I'm doing it.
我也不知道为什么，但现在我真的在做了。

1208
00:40:54.180 --> 00:40:55.220
I'm walking with this backpack.
我背着这个双肩包走路。

1209
00:40:55.500 --> 00:40:57.780
So people who are listening, they know it.
所以在听的各位都知道这回事。

1210
00:40:57.860 --> 00:40:59.640
And it's all, you know, bros do it.
而且这完全就是，你知道的，兄弟们才这么干。

1211
00:40:59.720 --> 00:41:03.960
And men who are taking too much testosterone in their sixties and they put this thing on
还有那些六十多岁摄入过多睾酮的男人们，他们背上这玩意儿

1212
00:41:03.960 --> 00:41:06.060
and it's all camouflage and black and flags.
全是迷彩、黑色和旗帜。

1213
00:41:06.200 --> 00:41:07.460
Oscar, we buy everything.
奥斯卡，我们什么都买。

1214
00:41:07.700 --> 00:41:08.220
I bought it.
我买的。

1215
00:41:08.220 --> 00:41:08.920
I bought everything.
我什么都买。

1216
00:41:09.120 --> 00:41:10.240
I have the weighted veSt.
我有负重背心。

1217
00:41:10.740 --> 00:41:15.080
Jenna, this morning, this made me laugh so hard this morning.
珍娜，今天早上，这事今天早上把我笑惨了。

1218
00:41:15.120 --> 00:41:16.000
This is so funny.
这太搞笑了。

1219
00:41:16.260 --> 00:41:20.940
I was on the gram today and I got fed this thing.
我今天刷照片墙的时候被推送了这个东西。

1220
00:41:21.180 --> 00:41:27.000
It is a neck massager that is supposedly going to tighten my jaw and jowls.
那是一个据说能收紧我的下巴和双下巴的颈部按摩仪。

1221
00:41:27.180 --> 00:41:29.600
So I want to read our text exchange this morning.
所以我想读一下我们今天早上的短信交流。

1222
00:41:29.780 --> 00:41:29.980
Okay.
好的。

1223
00:41:30.320 --> 00:41:32.440
I just, this is my first text to Angela this morning.
我只是，这是我今天早上给安吉拉发的第一条短信。

1224
00:41:32.440 --> 00:41:34.980
If you speak about it for five, 10 more minutes, I will buy it.
如果你再讲个五到十分钟，我就会买。

1225
00:41:35.120 --> 00:41:37.200
She's, this is at 947 AM.
她，这是在上午9点47分。

1226
00:41:37.540 --> 00:41:41.980
I just bought a neck massager off Instagram that is supposed to make your jawline look tighter
我刚从Instagram上买了一个颈部按摩仪，据说能让你的下巴线条看起来更紧致

1227
00:41:41.980 --> 00:41:42.740
without surgery.
而且不用做手术。

1228
00:41:44.020 --> 00:41:48.260
I, in my pajamas, pick it up because I've bought it, but she doesn't know.
我穿着睡衣把它拿起来，因为我已经买了，但她还不知道。

1229
00:41:48.460 --> 00:41:51.860
I take a selfie and I say, you mean this one that I bought last month?
我拍了张自拍然后说，你是说我上个月买的那款吗？

1230
00:41:52.560 --> 00:41:54.140
Fish in a barrel, ladies and gentlemen.
瓮中捉鳖啊，女士们先生们。

1231
00:41:54.380 --> 00:41:55.240
Same exact one.
完全一模一样的一款。

1232
00:41:55.240 --> 00:42:00.580
Send the office ladies, whatever crap they'll buy it and put it on their bodies.
送办公室辣妹们任何垃圾，她们都会买下来并戴在身上。

1233
00:42:00.800 --> 00:42:01.320
And me too.
我也是。

1234
00:42:01.320 --> 00:42:01.780
I shouldn't.
我不该这样的。

1235
00:42:02.180 --> 00:42:03.860
Jenna goes, oh my God, did it work?
珍娜说，天哪，有用吗？

1236
00:42:03.960 --> 00:42:07.620
I said, no, but you do get a very deep sort of shoulder massage.
我说，没有，但你确实能得到一种很深层的肩部按摩。

1237
00:42:07.660 --> 00:42:09.420
It's kind of painful for me.
对我来说有点疼。

1238
00:42:09.840 --> 00:42:11.620
She goes, have you been doing it every day?
她说，你每天都在用吗？

1239
00:42:11.780 --> 00:42:12.420
I said, no.
我说，没有。

1240
00:42:12.700 --> 00:42:14.500
Also, it doesn't really fit my neck.
而且，它其实不太适合我的脖子。

1241
00:42:15.120 --> 00:42:18.440
Josh is able to get a great neck rub out of it, but my neck is too small.
乔希能从它那得到很好的颈部按摩，但我的脖子太细了。

1242
00:42:18.440 --> 00:42:21.300
So instead it just squeezes the shit out of my shoulder.
所以它只好死死勒住我的肩膀。

1243
00:42:21.820 --> 00:42:23.540
So how did you hear about this?
那你怎么知道这个的？

1244
00:42:23.700 --> 00:42:25.760
It got fed to me on Instagram.
Instagram把它推送给我的。

1245
00:42:26.520 --> 00:42:27.540
Same with me.
我也是。

1246
00:42:27.540 --> 00:42:29.620
And I didn't tell you, but I had already bought it.
而且我没告诉你，我已经买过了。

1247
00:42:29.700 --> 00:42:35.240
I wish I texted you first, but I thought surely I'll be the first one to try it.
我真希望我先发短信给你，但我当时心想我肯定会是第一个尝试的人。

1248
00:42:35.240 --> 00:42:36.220
You took a chance.
你冒险了。

1249
00:42:36.340 --> 00:42:36.920
You took a chance.
你确实冒险了。

1250
00:42:37.280 --> 00:42:39.320
Let's get back to some fan questions here.
我们接下来看一些粉丝提问吧。

1251
00:42:39.500 --> 00:42:42.460
Oscar, you are a famous member of the Finer Things Club.
奥斯卡，你是“精致生活俱乐部”的知名成员。

1252
00:42:42.840 --> 00:42:43.600
So fun.
太有意思了。

1253
00:42:43.820 --> 00:42:46.540
And then the paper, you start making puzzles for the newspaper.
然后是纸张方面，你开始为报纸制作字谜游戏。

1254
00:42:46.540 --> 00:42:50.500
I mean, you win a whole award in the last episode for your puzzles.
我是说，你在上一集里还因为你的字谜赢得了大奖。

1255
00:42:51.700 --> 00:42:55.280
Are you into either of these things in real life, books or puzzles?
你在现实生活中喜欢这两样东西中的哪一样，书还是字谜？

1256
00:42:56.080 --> 00:42:56.880
I'm into books.
我喜欢书。

1257
00:42:57.620 --> 00:43:00.060
I'm into puzzles too, but I'm more into books than puzzles.
我也喜欢字谜，但我比字谜更喜欢书。

1258
00:43:00.280 --> 00:43:04.440
But the Finer Things Club, isn't it also art and the appreciation of art and music?
不过“精致生活俱乐部”，它不也涉及艺术以及对艺术和音乐的鉴赏吗？

1259
00:43:04.660 --> 00:43:05.560
It's a book club too.
它也是个读书俱乐部。

1260
00:43:05.680 --> 00:43:07.320
I feel like it's mainly a book club.
我觉得它主要还是个读书俱乐部。

1261
00:43:07.540 --> 00:43:09.720
I think so too, because we discussed the books.
我也这么觉得，因为我们探讨过书。

1262
00:43:09.900 --> 00:43:10.140
Yeah.
对。

1263
00:43:10.280 --> 00:43:10.620
Yeah.
对。

1264
00:43:10.940 --> 00:43:13.220
What are some books that you are liking right now?
你现在有什么喜欢的书吗？

1265
00:43:13.220 --> 00:43:14.180
Oh, I'm always...
哦，我总是……

1266
00:43:14.180 --> 00:43:15.000
Or that you've liked recently?
或者你最近喜欢过什么书？

1267
00:43:15.940 --> 00:43:16.840
Oh, let me see.
哦，让我想想。

1268
00:43:16.840 --> 00:43:20.640
I'm reading because I'm flying hither and thither.
我在阅读，因为我飞来飞去的。

1269
00:43:20.800 --> 00:43:25.200
And I have David Sedaris' new book, which is always good.
我带着大卫·塞德里亚斯的新书，他的书总是很好看。

1270
00:43:25.520 --> 00:43:26.420
I love David Sedaris.
我喜欢大卫·塞德里亚斯。

1271
00:43:26.420 --> 00:43:27.240
Yeah, he's very funny.
对，他非常有趣。

1272
00:43:27.280 --> 00:43:30.580
And it's easy for flights and stuff because it's short stories.
而且因为是短篇小说集，很适合在飞机上读之类的情况。

1273
00:43:31.100 --> 00:43:35.320
We were in Limerick, Ireland, and I picked up an Agatha Christie book just because I was there.
我们在爱尔兰利默里克时，我随手买了一本阿加莎·克里斯蒂的书，纯粹是因为我在那儿。

1274
00:43:35.560 --> 00:43:35.980
I love that.
我太喜欢这样了。

1275
00:43:35.980 --> 00:43:36.880
It's a murder mystery.
这是一个谋杀悬疑故事。

1276
00:43:36.900 --> 00:43:38.220
It's very silly and funny.
非常荒诞搞笑。

1277
00:43:38.420 --> 00:43:43.200
And I'll try to pass it on to August when I'm done with it.
我读完后会打算把它转交给奥古斯特。

1278
00:43:43.200 --> 00:43:46.740
Well, we thought this was a really good question from Megan B.
嗯，我们觉得这是梅根·B提出的一个非常好的问题。

1279
00:43:46.940 --> 00:43:48.400
from Silver Springs, Maryland.
她来自马里兰州银泉市。

1280
00:43:48.640 --> 00:43:50.500
This is kind of a thinking question, Oscar.
奥斯卡，这算是一个需要深思的问题。

1281
00:43:50.680 --> 00:43:51.240
I like Maryland.
我喜欢马里兰州。

1282
00:43:51.940 --> 00:43:56.320
Megan says, why is Oscar at Dunder Mifflin for so long?
梅根问，为什么奥斯卡在邓德米夫林呆了那么久？

1283
00:43:56.620 --> 00:44:03.040
Throughout the series, he seems to gain confidence and outspokenness and is clearly one of the more capable employees at the branch.
在整部剧里，他似乎越来越自信、敢于直言，显然是分公司里能力较强的员工之一。

1284
00:44:03.360 --> 00:44:05.780
So did Oscar have a backstory for staying?
那么对于留下来，奥斯卡有什么背景设定吗？

1285
00:44:07.080 --> 00:44:11.960
Oscar is unique and also the same as everyone else.
奥斯卡既是独特的，也和其他人一样。

1286
00:44:11.960 --> 00:44:13.600
Why do people stay where they are?
为什么人们会留在他们原来的地方？

1287
00:44:13.720 --> 00:44:14.740
They get complacent.
他们变得安于现状。

1288
00:44:15.280 --> 00:44:16.340
They get comfortable.
他们过得舒坦了。

1289
00:44:17.020 --> 00:44:19.180
You have a job every year.
你每年都有一份工作。

1290
00:44:19.220 --> 00:44:20.180
So you get a raise.
所以你会得到加薪。

1291
00:44:20.320 --> 00:44:21.160
Why take a chance?
为什么要冒风险呢？

1292
00:44:21.280 --> 00:44:22.100
Why go somewhere else?
为什么要跳槽去别的地方？

1293
00:44:22.700 --> 00:44:24.900
Know the devil you're with, whatever that saying is.
熟门熟路总好过重新适应，随便怎么说吧。

1294
00:44:25.060 --> 00:44:25.920
He's got friends.
他有朋友。

1295
00:44:25.920 --> 00:44:26.640
He's established.
他已经站稳脚跟了。

1296
00:44:27.060 --> 00:44:28.200
He's not 20.
他可不是20岁。

1297
00:44:28.360 --> 00:44:29.120
He's not 19.
他也不是19岁。

1298
00:44:29.480 --> 00:44:29.820
Right.
没错。

1299
00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:30.600
He's done.
他定型了。

1300
00:44:30.920 --> 00:44:31.520
He's comfortable.
他过得很安逸。

1301
00:44:31.720 --> 00:44:31.900
Yeah.
对。

1302
00:44:31.980 --> 00:44:35.100
If he went hunting, if something horrible happened, he's like, I got to get out of here.
如果他去打猎，如果发生了什么可怕的事，他会想，我得离开这里。

1303
00:44:35.460 --> 00:44:41.200
Then he'd get a resume and get a headhunter and do all that stuff that you have to do to find a good job somewhere else that's comparable.
然后他会弄一份简历，找个猎头，做所有那些你为了在别处找到一份待遇相当的好工作而必须做的事。

1304
00:44:41.200 --> 00:44:42.540
You don't want to go backwards.
你可不想走回头路。

1305
00:44:42.960 --> 00:44:43.120
Yeah.
没错。

1306
00:44:43.580 --> 00:44:49.120
And also, in this day and age, you're going to leave and go looking for a job and you're up against 20-year-olds and stuff?
而且在这个年代，你要是辞职去找工作，还得跟二十岁左右的小伙子们竞争什么的？

1307
00:44:49.640 --> 00:44:50.660
Yeah, at that age.
对，到了那年纪。

1308
00:44:50.660 --> 00:44:51.540
That's very true.
确实如此。

1309
00:44:51.600 --> 00:44:51.960
Yeah.
是啊。

1310
00:44:52.120 --> 00:44:55.220
You sort of hit a plateau and you're like, well, I've got a good thing.
你差不多到了一个瓶颈期，然后就会想，好吧，我现在这份挺不错的。

1311
00:44:55.420 --> 00:44:56.740
Jobs aren't easy to come by.
现在工作可不好找。

1312
00:44:56.740 --> 00:45:08.120
We did have a fun fan catch from Savannah Ellen, Virginia, who said this, during Suit Warehouse, Oscar can be seen typing an email to the New York PoSt.
我们收到了来自弗吉尼亚州萨凡纳·艾伦的一个有趣的粉丝来信，她是这么说的：在《西服仓库》那一集里，可以看到奥斯卡在给《纽约邮报》写电子邮件。

1313
00:45:08.440 --> 00:45:13.800
I paused to read the email and it said, quote, this would be the perfect job for me.
我暂停下来读了那封邮件，上面写着，引述一下：这对我来说将是完美的工作。

1314
00:45:13.800 --> 00:45:22.800
So, she spotted an instance of you maybe being interested in working at a paper before the paper.
所以，她发现了一个你可能在这家报社建立之前就对去那儿工作感兴趣的例子。

1315
00:45:22.960 --> 00:45:23.760
What's her name?
她叫什么名字？

1316
00:45:23.840 --> 00:45:24.260
Who is it?
是谁？

1317
00:45:24.500 --> 00:45:24.980
Savannah.
萨凡纳。

1318
00:45:25.440 --> 00:45:26.420
Savannah from Virginia.
来自弗吉尼亚的萨凡纳。

1319
00:45:26.420 --> 00:45:26.600
That's her name?
那是她的名字？

1320
00:45:26.980 --> 00:45:27.340
Yeah.
对。

1321
00:45:27.460 --> 00:45:27.940
Savannah.
萨凡纳。

1322
00:45:28.840 --> 00:45:30.060
You should be a PI.
你应该去当私家侦探。

1323
00:45:30.060 --> 00:45:32.100
I don't know what is happening.
我不知道这到底是怎么回事。

1324
00:45:32.400 --> 00:45:36.020
You have proof of me typing for another job?
你有我应聘其他工作的证据？

1325
00:45:36.180 --> 00:45:38.000
Yes, that this would be the perfect job for me.
对，就是“这对我来说将是完美的工作”。

1326
00:45:38.000 --> 00:45:47.980
And what I love about this is that there are always these moments in our rewatch of watching The Office, there would be some little thing that is background,
而我最喜欢这一点的是，在我们重看《办公室》的过程中，总会有这样一些时刻，背景里会有一些小细节，

1327
00:45:47.980 --> 00:45:54.060
that is like nothing of significance that the writers wrote or that you see on our desk.
那些编剧写进去的或者你在我们桌上看到的、根本微不足道的东西。

1328
00:45:54.200 --> 00:45:59.080
And then later, there's a whole storyline and fans will write in and say, was that foreshadowing?
然后过了一段时间，会有完整的一条故事线，粉丝们就会写信来问，那是伏笔吗？

1329
00:45:59.240 --> 00:46:03.980
Did they know five years before that Jim was going to play guitar or whatever it was?
他们是不是在五年之前就知道吉姆会弹吉他还是别的什么？

1330
